Dario, Gideon,

Re the either-or: it is the second one, and I would like it written down in t=
hose terms rather than left to whoever touches the check next. For me it only=
 counts as a stop when the marker is the tail end of what the model said. If =
the marker turns up mid-paragraph, the model is obviously still going, and th=
e only correct thing for the reader to do is carry on accumulating and leave =
the marker in the text as ordinary content. The two transcripts Gideon found =
are exactly that case and nothing else needs to be said about them =E2=80=94 =
the model was explaining our format, the marker had prose after it, so it was=
 never a stop.

That said, I want to be careful about what "tail end" means while a stream is=
 still open, because that is where I expect this to be got wrong. It means th=
e tail end of the model's output, not the tail end of whatever chunk happens =
to have arrived. A marker landing at the end of one chunk with three more chu=
nks behind it is mid-paragraph in every sense that matters to us; we simply h=
ave not seen the rest yet. So the position test only has an answer once the s=
tream has closed, and until then the reader has nothing to decide. If I had t=
o guess, that is the part that produced the original loose check in the first=
 place =E2=80=94 somebody wanted an answer per chunk and the only test that g=
ives one per chunk is the one that ignores position entirely. I wrote a fair =
amount of that reader on a late night and I would not swear it was reasoned a=
bout any harder than that at the time.

Dermot
